WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.750
PowerShell security starts with understanding the pieces you are using.

2
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:12.487
Scripts, modules, cmdlets, functions, pipelines, and objects all shape how data and actions move through automation.

3
00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:26.200
A script may look like plain text, but it runs in a real execution context with user identity, process environment, loaded modules, current directory, available commands, and access to local or remote resources.

4
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.418
The execution context matters as much as the code.

5
00:00:29.418 --> 00:00:40.679
A local script run in a standard user session has a different risk profile from a remote administrative session or a scheduled task running under a privileged service account.

6
00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:48.877
Administrative sessions should be deliberate, short-lived when possible, and scoped to the work that truly needs elevation.

7
00:00:48.877 --> 00:00:55.695
Running every script as an administrator is convenient, but it increases impact when the script is wrong.

8
00:00:55.695 --> 00:00:58.146
Remote execution adds another layer.

9
00:00:58.146 --> 00:01:10.327
PowerShell remoting can be valuable for controlled administration, but it also depends on authentication, session configuration, endpoint permissions, network access, and auditing.

10
00:01:10.327 --> 00:01:19.597
A script that is safe for one local workstation may behave differently when pushed across a fleet or run against cloud services with broad roles.

11
00:01:19.597 --> 00:01:28.100
Version differences between Windows PowerShell and modern PowerShell can also affect behavior and available security features.

12
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.701
Secure scripting depends on both code and environment.

13
00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:38.136
Good code validates parameters, avoids unsafe constructs, protects secrets, and handles errors.

14
00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:47.865
A good environment controls who can run the script, what identity it uses, which modules it loads, what systems it can reach, and how activity is logged.

15
00:01:47.865 --> 00:01:51.619
Treat those as one design problem, not separate concerns.
